<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Brazil</h1><p class="st-meta">1549 CE – 1815 CE · capital Salvador</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Portuguese royal government of Brazil set up at Salvador in 1549, which united the captaincies under a governor-general and later a viceroy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The hereditary captaincies of 1534 had largely failed, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">King John III</a> created a central government in 1549. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a> arrived as the first governor-general with soldiers, officials and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-da-nobrega">Manuel da Nóbrega</a>, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-all-saints">Bay of All Saints</a> as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The governor-general oversaw defence, justice and finance, while the captaincies kept their own governors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa">Mem de Sá</a> expelled the French from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/france-antarctique">France Antarctique</a>, and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estacio-de-sa">Estácio de Sá</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1565. In 1621 the north was separated as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-maranhao">State of Maranhão</a>, reporting directly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. From the 1640s governors often held the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>. A High Court, the Relação, sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> from 1609. The capital moved to Rio de Janeiro in 1763.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Portuguese court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1808, the colonial government was overshadowed by the monarchy itself. In 1815 the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> was raised to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-brazil.jpg" alt="State of Brazil" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: Shadowxfox · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brazil_in_1750.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1549 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salvador</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tomé de Sousa, Mem de Sá</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Brazil</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Governorate General of Brazil, Estado do Brasil</dd></dl></aside></div>
